-- ------------------------------------------------------------
-- Expire tenant-screening invitations that have run out of time
--
-- NOT APPLIED. The `.pending` extension is deliberate and follows the two jobs
-- already parked beside this one: a migration run ignores this file until it is
-- renamed to `.sql`, and it should not be renamed until step 2 below is done,
-- because the route refuses every request while its secret is unset. Switching
-- the schedule on first would produce a job that runs every hour, is refused
-- every hour, and tells nobody.
--
-- WHAT IT DOES, AND WHY IT IS THE SAFEST OF THE THREE
--
-- Moves a screening order from `invited` to `expired` once its invitation has
-- lapsed — the provider's own deadline when there is one, otherwise fourteen
-- days from the invitation. That is the whole of its authority. It cannot
-- complete an order, cannot reach a report, and touches no money. Compare the
-- on-call sweep parked next to it, which rings people's phones.
--
-- It is idempotent: a second run finds nothing, because the first moved those
-- orders out of `invited`. Overlapping schedules are harmless.
--
-- BEFORE RENAMING THIS TO .sql
--
-- 1. Nothing to do about `private_cron_config`, and the alarming version of
--    this item was wrong — including the version that stood in this file for
--    an hour on 2026-10-03.
--
--    The on-call migration beside this one described the table as "readable and
--    writable by anyone holding the publishable key". It never was: `anon` and
--    `authenticated` hold no grants on it at all, so the publishable key cannot
--    reach it through the REST API. Verified against production, and the
--    on-call file has since been corrected with the same finding.
--
--    Row-level security has also now been enabled on the table as a second
--    lock. Both roles that matter here bypass it — `postgres`, which owns the
--    cron jobs, and `service_role`, which the edge function connects as.
--
--    So: no secret was exposed and nothing needs rotating. This file first
--    claimed `compliance_cron_secret` should be rotated "whether or not anybody
--    read it", which was advice built on the same bad premise.
--
-- 2. Put this job's secret in Vault, which is encrypted and not reachable with
--    the publishable key:
--
--        select vault.create_secret(
--          encode(gen_random_bytes(32), 'base64'),
--          'screening_cron_secret',
--          'Authorises POST /cron/screening/expire-invitations'
--        );
--
-- 3. Set the SAME value as the edge-function secret `SCREENING_CRON_SECRET` in
--    the Supabase dashboard. Read it back with:
--
--        select decrypted_secret from vault.decrypted_secrets
--        where name = 'screening_cron_secret';
--
--    The route also accepts the secret from `private_cron_config` under the key
--    `screening_cron_secret`, which is how the autopilot tick does it. Vault is
--    the better of the two and this is the job to start the habit on, since it
--    has no history to preserve.
--
-- WHY HOURLY
--
-- Because the unit here is days. An invitation that lapsed at 3am does not need
-- noticing at 3:01, and a landlord looking at their list wants it to have been
-- noticed by the time they look. Hourly is well inside that and costs one scan
-- of the orders.
-- ------------------------------------------------------------

select cron.schedule(
  'screening-invitation-expiry',
  '7 * * * *',
  $$
  select net.http_post(
    url := 'https://plzsvzwwcdopnawtiwzm.supabase.co/functions/v1/make-server-3eae23a6/cron/screening/expire-invitations',
    headers := jsonb_build_object(
      'Content-Type', 'application/json',
      -- The gateway wants an Authorization header before the request reaches
      -- our code at all; the publishable key satisfies it and authorises
      -- nothing. The header below is what actually authorises this.
      'Authorization', 'Bearer ' || (select value from private_cron_config where key = 'anon_key'),
      'X-Screening-Cron-Secret', (select decrypted_secret from vault.decrypted_secrets where name = 'screening_cron_secret')
    ),
    body := '{}'::jsonb
  );
  $$
);

-- To stop it again:
--   select cron.unschedule('screening-invitation-expiry');
--
-- To see what it has been doing:
--   select * from cron.job_run_details
--   where jobid = (select jobid from cron.job where jobname = 'screening-invitation-expiry')
--   order by start_time desc limit 20;
